  <static seed="355" source="Library/Michigan/Chap14Sec7/Q05.pg">
    <statement><p>Calculate all
four second-order partial derivatives and check that <m>f_{xy}=f_{yx}</m>.
Assume the variables are restricted to a domain on which the function
is defined.
<me>f(x,y) = e^{3xy}</me></p>
<p><m>f_{xx} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>f_{yy} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>f_{xy} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><m>f_{yx} =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>9y^{2}e^{3xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>9x^{2}e^{3xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3e^{3xy}+9xye^{3xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3e^{3xy}+9xye^{3xy}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f_x = 3ye^{3xy}</m> and <m>f_y = 3xe^{3xy}</m>, so
<me>f_{xx} = 9y^{2}e^{3xy}, f_{yy} = 9x^{2}e^{3xy},</me>
and
<me>f_{xy} = 3e^{3xy}+9xye^{3xy} = f_{yx} = 3e^{3xy}+9xye^{3xy}.</me>



</solution>
